From: Pat Wendorf <dungeons@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] screencopy: wake a frame's waiter only after the frame is destroyed

cosmic-client-toolkit's frame dispatch calls ready()/failed() and only
then destroy()s the ext_image_copy_capture_frame_v1. ready()/failed()
sent the result straight to the capture thread waiting in
capture_wl_buffer(), so that thread could send create_frame for the next
capture before the dispatch thread had queued the old frame's destroy.
The compositor then sees two live frames on one session, raises
ext_image_copy_capture_session_v1 error 1 (duplicate_frame), and the
wayland thread panics and takes the whole portal down: every screencast
and remote-desktop session dies with it. It takes a frame that completes
while PipeWire already has the next buffer queued, so it shows up after
a few minutes of real use (RDP on behobasement, 2026-11-02, twice).

Queue the results in AppData and send them after blocking_dispatch()
returns. By then each finished frame's destroy is in the connection's
outgoing buffer, so whatever the woken thread sends is ordered after it.

Upstream: not offered; carried
Signed-off-by: Pat Wendorf <dungeons@gmail.com>
---
--- a/src/wayland/mod.rs
+++ b/src/wayland/mod.rs
@@ -123,6 +123,28 @@
     toplevel_info_state: ToplevelInfoState,
     workspace_state: WorkspaceState,
     seat_state: SeatState,
+    // PengyOS: frame results to hand to their waiters once the dispatch that
+    // produced them has returned (see `send_finished_frames`).
+    #[allow(clippy::type_complexity)]
+    finished_frames: Vec<(
+        oneshot::Sender<Result<Frame, WEnum<FailureReason>>>,
+        Result<Frame, WEnum<FailureReason>>,
+    )>,
+}
+
+impl AppData {
+    /// cosmic-client-toolkit calls `ready`/`failed` *before* it destroys the frame
+    /// object. Waking the capture thread from there lets it send `create_frame` for
+    /// the next capture ahead of that `destroy`, and the compositor kills the
+    /// connection with ext_image_copy_capture_session_v1 error 1 (duplicate_frame).
+    /// After `blocking_dispatch` returns, every finished frame's `destroy` is already
+    /// in the connection's outgoing buffer, so any request a woken thread sends is
+    /// queued after it.
+    fn send_finished_frames(&mut self) {
+        for (sender, result) in self.finished_frames.drain(..) {
+            let _ = sender.send(result);
+        }
+    }
 }
 
 impl Drop for AppData {
@@ -327,14 +349,18 @@
             toplevel_info_state: ToplevelInfoState::new(&registry_state, &qh),
             registry_state,
             seat_state: SeatState::new(&globals, &qh),
+            finished_frames: Vec::new(),
         };
         event_queue.flush().unwrap();
 
         event_queue.roundtrip(&mut data).unwrap();
+        data.send_finished_frames();
 
         thread::spawn(move || {
             loop {
-                match event_queue.blocking_dispatch(&mut data) {
+                let result = event_queue.blocking_dispatch(&mut data);
+                data.send_finished_frames();
+                match result {
                     Ok(_) => {}
                     // The connection is still usable; skip the malformed message
                     Err(err @ DispatchError::BadMessage { .. }) => {
@@ -741,7 +767,7 @@
             .data::<FrameData>()
             .and_then(|data| data.sender.lock().unwrap().take())
         {
-            let _ = sender.send(Ok(frame));
+            self.finished_frames.push((sender, Ok(frame)));
         }
     }
 
@@ -756,7 +782,7 @@
             .data::<FrameData>()
             .and_then(|data| data.sender.lock().unwrap().take())
         {
-            let _ = sender.send(Err(reason));
+            self.finished_frames.push((sender, Err(reason)));
         }
     }
 
